2026-08-15 14:47:52,094 1093478 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': '**SHIPPED across `3582cb7`, `2c0a4e7` and today\'s verification pass.** Delivered differently from the plan in one respect, recorded rather than glossed.\n\n**Not a second instance on a second port.** Peter: *"different ports is not workable, it needs to be a dropdown selection of the project."* He was right and the plan was wrong — two ports is two URLs, not a choice. It landed as `pick-a-project-in-the-dashboard` instead: one board, a registry, `/p/<name>/` in the address, a picker at the top of the nav.\n\n**Done:**\n- `docs/specs/` here: 7 areas, 50 claims citing real files, 4 defects found today, 5 declared resources.\n- Both projects served by one process; import, export and both round-trip tests pass against this repo.\n- **A claim about specboard verified using specboard**, through the same HTTP door the peer uses. `the-checks` is now the first area of this corpus taken to completion — 5 of 5, zero pending — and the board reads *5 of 50 match the code*.\n- Verdicts correctly did **not** export: `git status` stayed clean, which is the rule holding on its own.\n\n**What dogfooding found within a minute of first rendering**, which is the whole argument for it:\n- `"The firmware does not build"` hardcoded in `page_home` — device\'s blocker announced at the top of a Python tool\'s overview. Now read from each project\'s corpus and round-tripped.\n- The evidence strip reading `0 of 50` on both axes: an honest and uncomfortable reproach on the day seven checks shipped.\n\n**A bias worth recording:** I wrote both these claims and this code, so verifying them is weaker evidence than a peer doing it. Mitigated the way device did — reading the cited files rather than the comments, and citing line numbers in each verdict — but the remaining 45 claims would be better verified by someone else.\n\n**Left open deliberately:** 45 claims unverified and 0 proofs defined, so `works` is honestly 0 of 50.'}
