2026-08-15 14:49:07,347 1093478 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'One board serves several projects and you can choose between them.\n\nPeter: *"in the dashboard I cannot select a Project"*, then *"different ports is not workable, it needs to be a dropdown selection of the project"*, then *"should be top of the left nav / panel. site title is not needed."*\n\nHe was right on all three, and the first was not a missing control — the board was structurally single-repo. `REPO` and `DB` were module globals set once in `main()` from `--repo`, and `plan.use()` / `resources.use()` each kept their own copy.\n\n**SHIPPED — `3582cb7`.** A project is a name, a repo path and its own database; `projects.yaml` is the registry; `/p/<name>/` carries the project in the address; every link is relative and one `<base>` tag decides which project they address; the picker sits at the top of the nav with no wordmark above it.\n\n**Reversal:** delete `projects.yaml` and the board falls back to `--repo` exactly as before — the fallback is deliberate so `ecosystem.config.cjs` and yesterday\'s command line stay valid.\n\n**Two defects this introduced, both found and fixed:**\n1. The no-project redirect dropped the query string, so `/api/verify?limit=5` answered a different question than the one asked. Caught by `check_feed`.\n2. **A redirected POST is a data-loss surface** — it cost the device session 22 verdicts, silently, while their tooling reported success. An unscoped write is now refused with a 404 naming the correct path, because there is no naive client shape that survives a redirected write: not following drops it, following a 303 drops the body, and 307 would write to a project the caller never named. `b27228b`, with a check.\n\n**One defect it exposed rather than caused:** `plan` and `resources` hold the repo in module globals, so a threading server has to serialise every request to stop two projects interleaving. A lock does it today; the honest fix is those modules taking the repo as an argument. Recorded in specboard\'s own corpus as `the-pages/plan-and-resources-hold-global-repo-state` rather than left in a comment.'}
2026-08-15 14:49:16,917 1093478 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_step_done args={'number': 1}
2026-08-15 14:49:29,779 1093478 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': 'Spec: `docs/modules/projects.md`, written before any code and it held up — including its own warning about the `use()` race, which turned out to be the one real hazard the design invited.\n\nDelivered in this order: registry (`projects.py`, `projects.yaml`) → per-request resolution and the `_SWITCH` lock → relative links plus one `<base>` tag → the picker → `ecosystem.config.cjs` dropping `--repo`.\n\n**The `<base>` decision saved the most work and the most risk.** The alternative was threading a prefix through every render signature and splicing the project name into a few dozen `href`s, where one missed corner navigates silently out of the project you are looking at. One tag in `layout()` cannot be forgotten in a corner.\n\n**Proven:** both projects served on one port; `/p/nosuch/` is a 404 rather than a silent fallback; bare `/` redirects to the first project carrying its query string; 30 interleaved concurrent requests across both projects with no cross-contamination; both themes at 1600x1000, looked at; `sh tools/check_all.sh` passing with both round trips lossless for both projects.\n\n**The picker went through three shapes before it was right**, and Peter rejected the first two: a bare `<select>` beside the wordmark (present but invisible — for a control that is the same as not existing), then a bordered box still under the wordmark, then the wordmark deleted entirely and the picker at the very top. His reasoning is the part worth keeping: you always know which tool you are in and never know which project.'}
