2026-08-15 16:45:23,383 1093478 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_new args={'name': 'cut-work-from-a-defect', 'title': 'GAP - the board says cut work from N defects and provides no way to cut work', 'steps': ['IMPLEMENT the route, the form, and the link from a defect to its task', 'OBSERVE - cut work from the one-database defect in a browser and watch the next action change', 'TRACK - commit']}
2026-08-15 16:45:36,003 1093478 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'goal', 'text': 'A defect Peter has accepted can be linked to the mcpp task that does the work.\n\nFound by using the board to manage a change to the board — Peter asked *"as this is dogfood how is that going to be managed / controlled?"*, so the one-database decision was raised as a defect on specboard\'s own board and ruled `accepted`. The board immediately answered *"accepted, no work cut yet"* and offered no way to cut it.\n\n`defect.task` exists in `schema.sql`, commented *"mcpp task name, once work is cut"*. **Nothing writes it.** `_next_action` renders `cut work from %d defects` as the instruction for a whole area, and there is no route, no form and no store function behind it.\n\n**This is the third instance of one family today**, which is the part worth recording: `rule_defect` had no caller, nothing could create a defect at all, and now nothing can schedule one. Each time the schema modelled the step and no code performed it, and each time it was invisible until somebody tried to do the thing.\n\nDone means: a defect can be linked to a task from the page, the area\'s next action stops saying cut work, and the link is visible on the defect so a reader can get from the problem to the work.'}
2026-08-15 16:45:41,481 1093478 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'kind': 'plan', 'text': '**Files:** `db.py` (`schedule_defect`), `app.py` (`POST /defects/schedule`), `render.py` (the control and the link), `export.py` + importer already handle `task`, `tools/check_defects.py`.\n\n`task` already exports and imports — that was fixed earlier today when the importer was found not to read `wont_why` or `task` back. So this needs no round-trip work, and `check_roundtrip` will confirm it.\n\n**Only an `accepted` defect can be scheduled.** Cutting work from something nobody has ruled on inverts the order the whole triage surface exists to enforce: raising is not ruling, and ruling is not scheduling.\n\n**The task name is not validated against mcpp.** Tempting, and wrong for now: the board asks mcpp for what is active and must not become a second place that decides what a task is. A typo produces a dead link rather than a wrong fact, which is the cheaper failure. Worth revisiting if it bites.\n\n**Reversal:** additive — one column already present, one function, one route, one form.'}
