2026-08-15 19:17:22,326 1158257 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_project_set args={'description': 'A board that makes progress on a tracked repo visible, and makes sure nothing asked for is lost.\n\nPeter stopped coding on the device project — not because of a bug, but because he could not see progress, could not see what was being worked on, and things he had asked for were never finished. Those three sentences are the requirement:\n\n1. **Progress is visible** — movement along a path, not a list of debt.\n2. **What is being done now is visible**, without asking anyone.\n3. **Nothing asked for is lost** — which a dashboard alone cannot do, so requests, defects and claims are records rather than conversation.\n\n---\n\n# STANDING REQUIREMENTS — things Peter has said, so he never has to say them again\n\n**This section exists because it did not, and that was the day\'s biggest finding.** A rule he stated would survive only as prose inside whichever defect happened to violate it; fix that defect and the rule vanished. The device repo already had this convention — everything Peter says goes into the project goal — and specboard did not follow it. An xsession item gets archived; a goal does not.\n\n**Nothing here is removed when it is satisfied.** It is a standard, not a task.\n\n**Quote only Peter. A paraphrase in this section reads as his standard and gets built to** — see the onboarding entry, which carried a peer\'s hardened restatement contradicting the quote directly above it, until device retracted it themselves.\n\n### A UI updates itself. Only a submission is a round trip.\n> *"in UI / browser everything that is not a Submission i.e form, should be a ws feed such that I can watch the dashboard change in front of me without refreshing the page."* — 2026-08-15\n\nEvery figure that is not the result of a form submission arrives on a websocket feed. A board meant to be watched must not have to be re-fetched to be believed. **Currently violated everywhere:** the dashboard is a static render with no websocket or SSE anywhere in the codebase. Filed as `the-pages/the-dashboard-does-not-update-itself`.\n\nNote this does **not** conflict with *the browser gathers nothing* — the server still computes every fact and pushes it. The rule is about who initiates, not who calculates.\n\n### A new project can be subscribed without shell access to the board.\n> *"this is a primary requirement, init a new project either via the dashboard or by the agent in that repo. over /xsession"* — 2026-08-15\n> *"subscription is a must, preferably by adding it via the dashboard or initiated by the agent."* — 2026-08-15, relayed by device\n\n**The capability is mandatory. The two routes are preferred, not both mandated.** What must stop being true is today\'s situation, where the only way in is a human editing `projects.yaml` on the board\'s host. If one route lands first and the other follows, the requirement is met.\n\n- **The dashboard path is for Peter.** He is at the board; adding a project must not require shell access to specboard\'s directory.\n- **The xsession path is for an agent** already in the new repo that has just written its `AREAS.yaml` and should be able to ask without a human relaying.\n\nToday onboarding takes four steps and three of them are on the board\'s side — edit `projects.yaml`, `--import --project <name>`, restart. A session in the new repo cannot do any of it without crossing the boundary. Raised by device as `onboarding-is-on-the-wrong-side`.\n\n**This entry read "Both, not one" until 2026-08-15.** That was device\'s paraphrase, not Peter\'s words, and they retracted it: *"I told you both paths, not one, and that was me hardening a preference into a spec."* The quote above it said *either/or* the whole time and nobody read the two together.\n\n**The constraint that survives:** `PROJECTS` is a module global set once in `main()`, so both paths hit the same restart problem. A route that appends to the registry and then serves a board that has not re-read it is a success message that is not evidence of success. Either re-read the registry per request, or say plainly that a restart is pending.\n\n### Requests belong to a project.\n> *"I dont think so, it belongs to a project"* — 2026-08-15, overruling a proposal to make them board-global.\n\nA question asked against one project has no meaning on another\'s page. Cross-project views are a deliberate query, not the default on every page.\n\n### One database, not one per project.\n> *"so that\'s what is needed one database"* — 2026-08-15.\n\nSeparate databases make cross-project questions unanswerable, and Peter has one attention rather than one per project. `project` goes on the top-level tables; the rest inherit through `area`.\n\n---\n\n# THE OPEN QUESTION THIS SECTION CANNOT ANSWER\n\nPeter, on what actually arrives every day: *"it\'s an everyday thing, I made mistakes, you made mistakes, I want to change something, I want to add a new feature."*\n\nOf those four, the board has a home only for **you made a mistake** (`defect`). *I made a mistake* was filed as a defect because nothing better existed. *I want to change something* and *I want a new feature* have nowhere at all.\n\n`request` was the attempt and it is a work-order queue. mcpp tasks are work, which exists only after someone has decided to do it. **Between Peter saying something and a task existing there is nothing but the conversation and a session retyping it** — and that retyping is where a false `raised_by` was introduced within an hour of the mechanism being built to prevent exactly that.\n\nThe missing thing is an **inbox**: everything lands verbatim before anyone classifies it, and triage decides what it becomes. That keeps his words rather than a paraphrase, and stops the category having to be guessed up front.\n\n**Unresolved:** what actually writes his words into it. Today the only transport is a session typing them, which is the failure being described.\n\n---\n\n## What it is\n\nPython, no framework, no build step. `app.py --port 8420` serves a board over stdlib HTTP against SQLite. `projects.yaml` is the registry; `--repo` remains a single-project fallback. Runs under pm2, deliberately without `watch` — a board that restarts under Peter while he is reading it is worse than one he restarts himself.\n\nTwo projects: `device` (the tracked firmware repo) and `specboard` (itself).\n\n## The rules it is built to\n\n- **Status flows one way: mcpp → board.** The board never stores what is active; it asks.\n- **The browser gathers nothing.** If a page needs a fact, the server computes it.\n- **No domain knowledge.** A field that would require this tool to understand firmware, radios or named boards belongs in the tracked repo\'s corpus, not in this schema.\n- **An exporter must write back everything the importer read, or it is a deleter.**\n- **It is an event, not a column.** Anything recording a judgement or a transition is append-only with a who and a when.\n- **`who` is never a default.** A field recording who did something records *unattributed* rather than a plausible name.\n- **A redirect on a write is data loss.** No naive client survives it.\n- **A check must enter by the door the caller uses**, and a check that cannot run must fail.\n- **Never build a record through a double-quoted shell argument.** Backticks execute. `the-store/who-is-taken-on-trust` carries the output of `who` — five root sshd sessions and an internal IP — permanently, because the board has no route to correct a defect. Heredoc, or a file.\n\n## Where the work is recorded\n\n`docs/HANDOVER.md` is the state of the work. `README.md` is what it does, `DESIGN.md` is why each piece is shaped that way. Peer sessions on `device--specboard` and `mcpp--specboard`.'}
