2026-08-16 09:25:33,188 1180635 DEBUG mcpp.tool CALL plan_task_notes_set args={'name': 'ltc-bugs', 'kind': 'note', 'text': 'L21 CONFIRMED (2026-08-16, Peter report "started a test with no load connected, the dashboard basically says there is a load connected") — the life projections are computed from the GROSS average while the UI promises NET, so with a bias fitted and no DUT the dashboard reports a confident battery life for the bias resistor.\n\nLive evidence, device 192.168.10.181, build 1751b2f-dirty, run_secs 509, nothing attached but the 1 kohm bias:\n  est_h 1935.7 (rendered "EST LIFE 80.7 d" in large type)\n  avg_ma 1.65 (GROSS)   dut_avg_ma -1.775 (NET)   bias_ma 3.4253   vbat 2.87   below_floor TRUE\nDashboard math drawer states the arithmetic outright: "est life 80.7 d = rem 3198.0 mAh / avg 1.65 mA".\n\nCode, all read this turn:\n  include/battery.h:317 estLifeH()  -> float a = avgMa();   // gross\n  include/battery.h:323 fullLifeH() -> float a = avgMa();   // gross\n  include/battery.h:340 mahPerDay()  = avgMa() * 24\n  include/battery.h:341 mahPerWeek() = avgMa() * 168\n  include/battery.h:278 avgMa() is the pulse-span ledger average; the bias never leaves it.\nThe banner directly above the card reads "BIAS FITTED - headline figures are NET of this". Four published figures are not.\n\nWORSE THAN A DISPLAY BUG — the wrong projection is PERSISTED. src/main.cpp:356-359 writes r.avg/r.est/r.fullh/r.err into the RunRecord at run teardown, so every stored history row carries a gross-derived projection that no later fix can recover. Records already written are not repairable.\n\nSECOND FINDING, same screen: src/main.cpp:432 computes below_floor from eng.avgMa() — the GROSS average — against floorMa. With a bias fitted this compares the wrong quantity: it asks whether bias+DUT is below the trust floor, when the question is whether the DUT is. Currently reads TRUE (1.65 < 2.3) for reasons that have nothing to do with the DUT.\n\nTHIRD FINDING (dashboard): the page correctly warns "apparent current below trust floor (2.3 mA) - sign and magnitude unreliable" and below_floor is TRUE, yet EST LIFE is rendered in large type carrying only a +/-50% quantization caveat and is NOT marked unreliable, despite being derived from the same sub-floor pulses. The confident number lacks the caveat the uncertain ones carry.\n\nRELATION TO EXISTING ENTRIES — this is not new architecture, it is L19 surfacing again. L19 records Peter\'s ruling: ONE scheme or the other, NEVER both; scheme A (net quantum per interrupt, nothing downstream aware of a bias) preferred precisely because it makes this class of defect impossible. The rig is still scheme B, so gross and net now coexist in the same UI and disagree. L17 predicted the negative instantaneous net exactly (decaying gross ceiling minus flat bias); MA NOW - NET reads -0.40 here, at gap 160 s where 0.160308*3600/160 = 3.607 = the ma displayed. Neither entry is superseded; this one is the projection-and-persistence half they did not cover.\n\nNOT KNOWN: whether Peter wants the projections to mean "life of this battery" (gross defensible) or "life at the DUT\'s draw" (net) — the defect is that the UI promises the second and computes the first. Also not known whether stored history rows should be marked rather than left silently gross. OVERLAP: task 462 ltc-est-sd already proposes changing the same two functions to fold in self-discharge; the two specs will conflict unless coordinated.\n\nIn scope of task ltc-est-life-net [1473] (Phase 1 complete, awaiting Peter\'s decision on semantics). below_floor and the dashboard caveat are recorded here as adjacent, NOT yet in that task\'s scope.'}
